alexandre baghanya never knows when the  next prison riot will break out  she lives across the street from  guayaquil's infamous state penitentiary  from over there you can hear the victim  screams the cries for help it's  terrifying and when the prisoners  explode dynamite you jump from the bed  late last year it took police days to  enter the penitentiary after 118 inmates  were hacked to death and set on fire in  a battle between rival drug gangs  ecuador has become a sanctuary for the  multi-billion dollar narcotics trade  it's run from prisons like this one by  local drug lords  but also by international criminal  organizations like albania's  companiabello  its leader is alleged to be controlling  the export of tons of cocaine from this  ecuadorian jail where he's serving a  13-year sentence  it all starts here  we went on a patrol with special forces  police in guayaquil where gang violence  is  soaring neighborhoods like this one  provide the cannon fodder for criminal  organizations people who live in poverty  and who have little hope of ever finding  a dignified job are willing and able to  help criminal organizations from abroad  export their wares from ecuador to the  rest of the world  ecuador's geographic position and the  use of the us dollar for its currency  make it ideal for drug cartels  luscious tropical fruits especially  bananas are exported from guayaquil  specific ports to europe asia and russia  so far this year more than 300 tons of  cocaine have been confiscated in cargo  but ecuadorian experts estimate that's  less than 30 percent of what gets  through  small fishing boats like these and  homemade submarines also take colombian  cocaine north  it's a short trip from guayaquil to  central america and mexico from where  the sinaloa and gulf cartels take the  cargo across the u.s border  security minister diego ordonez tells us  that years of permissiveness tolerance  and corruption have made all this  possible  the problem is that the narcotics trade  has penetrated politics it's penetrated  the administration of justice that's why  i say we are facing a serious threat to  the very stability of the state  the government has imposed a month-long  state of emergency in the guayaquil area  but that will clearly be insufficient to  address the crisis much less the social  problems that nourish it  lucia newman al jazeera guayaquil  ecuador
 

